\paragraph{Relation to interpretability without actionability.}
\citet{basu2026interpretability} reported that steering failed in clinical NLP tasks despite near-perfect probe accuracy (98.2\% area under the receiver operating characteristic (AUROC)).
Our results present a partial contrast: mid-layer steering succeeds with a weaker probe signal, but the key difference is layer selection---\citeauthor{basu2026interpretability} steered at layers selected for probe accuracy, whereas our L28 (probe peak) also fails, with causal influence found only at L13.

\paragraph{Direct evidence for a shared cross-lingual subspace.}
A language identity probe (EN vs.\ ZH) trained on conflict task activations and evaluated on XNLI activations achieves $\geq 0.96$ accuracy across all three models, confirming that both tasks encode language identity in a common representational subspace.
At Llama L13, conflict$\to$XNLI transfer is 0.998 [0.993, 1.000]; Gemma shows reduced mid-layer transfer at L21 (0.730) but L17 remains near-perfect (0.994) despite steering failure, suggesting that subspace disruption is not the sole factor behind negative steering outcomes.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We showed that knowledge conflict signals in multilingual LLMs are linearly detectable across languages (balanced accuracies 75.0--77.6\% within-language, all cross-lingual transfers above chance at $p < 0.001$) and that the transfer asymmetry (ZH$\to$EN exceeding EN$\to$ZH by ${\sim}$6--7~pp) mirrors the XNLI non-conflict baseline across all three architectures, reflecting general cross-lingual structure rather than conflict-specific encoding.
In Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, causal influence is consistently localized to L13 (41\% depth; Fisher $p = 0.002$) regardless of where detection accuracy peaks, and all late-layer interventions fail, demonstrating that linear detectability is neither sufficient nor predictive of causal relevance.
Steering failed in both Qwen and Gemma despite Gemma's 88.9\% probe accuracy, confirming architecture-specific causal influence.
Future work should explore alternative intervention methods, diverse language pairs, and sparse autoencoder decomposition.

\paragraph{Conflict signals are encoded in a general cross-lingual space.}
The ZH$\to$EN transfer advantage is consistent across all three architectures ($+7.3$, $+7.2$, $+6.6$~pp) and matches the XNLI baseline ($+6.0$, $+6.7$, $+7.8$~pp), indicating that the asymmetry is not conflict-specific but piggybacks on pre-existing cross-lingual alignment \citep{zhao2026when, kargaran2024mexa, wendler2024llamas}.
Any cross-lingual probing study must account for this baseline asymmetry before attributing transfer differences to task-specific phenomena.

